            <ArticleTitle>Basic Electrophysiologic Mechanisms of Sudden Cardiac Death Caused by Acute Myocardial Ischemia and Infarction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>525-536</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1877-9182(17)30090-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ccep.2017.07.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Sudden cardiac death caused by acute ischemia results from electrophysiologic changes in myocardium deprived of its blood supply. These changes include a reduction in resting potential and phase 0 depolarization and an increase in intercellular resistivity that slow conduction, cause conduction block, and lead to reentrant excitation and ventricular fibrillation. Reperfusion of a coronary artery after a short period of occlusion leads to similar changes.</AbstractText>
